  "knowledgePattern": "Count the admissible tangent branches after resolving the principal root",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Rewrite the square root",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Sketch the sine curve over the given interval, marking its zeros to decide where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is positive and where it is negative.</p>",
      "recap": "The square root of a square is the absolute value.",
      "pitfall": "Do not replace the square root by sin(2x) everywhere.",
      "pause": "Write the equation separately on the two halves of the interval.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "Keep the original sign when removing a square root or modulus."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn each case into a tangent equation",
      "body": "<p>Check inputs where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in the original equation. At other inputs, divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Sine divided by cosine is tangent.",
      "pitfall": "Division is valid only where the cosine is nonzero.",
      "pause": "Write the resulting tangent equation for each case.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b2-l04",
          "reminder": "Check the denominator before dividing a trigonometric equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Draw each branch separately",
      "body": "<p>Mark the tangent asymptotes and the endpoints of each case. On the same axes, draw the straight line from that case.</p>",
      "recap": "Each crossing gives one solution.",
      "pitfall": "Do not join a tangent curve across an asymptote or count a forbidden endpoint.",
      "pause": "Count all crossings on the permitted branches and check the dividing points.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-graphical-solutions",
          "reminder": "An equation is solved where its two graphs meet."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The left side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the two sides are 1 and 0, so these inputs are not solutions.</p><p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, sine is positive and the equation becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the tangent is positive while the line is negative, so there is no crossing. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the tangent rises from negative infinity to 0 and crosses the line once.</p><p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>, sine is negative. Write the equation as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the tangent rises from 0 to positive infinity while the line falls to 0: one crossing. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the tangent rises from negative infinity to 0 while the line is negative and decreasing: one crossing.</p><p>For the first rising-line comparison, the tangent curve is steeper than the line. Here is a geometric check, using radians. Take two angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. The rays at those angles meet the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math> contains the unit-circle sector between the rays. Their areas are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Shifting both angles by a multiple of π gives the same result on any tangent branch. With input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, tangent therefore gains more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, while a line of gradient 1 gains only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. The rising-line crossing is unique.</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, substitution in the original gives 0 on the left and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> on the right, so it adds no solution. The endpoints 0 and π are excluded. There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> solutions: D.</p>"
}
